// @log 350
// The SAME program as `union-box-payload-read` / `union-sink-if-expression` /
// `union-sink-let-if`, in the FOURTH spelling: a mutable `let` written on two paths by
// SEPARATE STATEMENTS, with the payload CONSUMED (`u as i32`) rather than discarded.
//
// This is the documented LIMIT of the union-box melt, and it is pinned with the payload
// read because that is the only way to see it. `union-box-branch-local` discards the
// payload, so at `-O3` closed-world field removal deletes the box's payload field
// outright and its count falls 4 -> 2 for a reason that has nothing to do with the box
// melting. Read here, the field cannot be removed and the shape reads 4/4/4: NOTHING
// melts, at any rung, including the full release profile.
//
// WHY, measured rather than reasoned (a hand-built WAT A/B on this exact function):
// the two writes are separate statements, so the `(ref $uBox)` local has TWO definitions.
// Heap2Local scalarizes per allocation SITE and an allocation can only be scalarized when
// every local it reaches provably holds that one allocation, so neither site can own the
// local and neither melts. Nothing escapes — the identical function with the tag and
// payload held in two scalar locals across the branch and ONE `struct.new` after it melts
// the box at plain `-O` and reads 3/2/2, the same as the three sinkable spellings.
//
// So the blocker is neither an escape (the value never leaves the frame) nor a missing
// control-flow marker in the emitter (`ctrlEnter`/`ctrlLeave` count wasm frame DEPTH for
// `br` operands; they are invisible to binaryen). It is the single-definition requirement
// of Heap2Local, and routing around it means the emitter keeping a union-typed local's tag
// and payload in scalars across a LIVENESS WINDOW and materialising the box at its end —
// local scalarization, a REPRESENTATION change, not a peephole. That is
// `unboxed-union-rep-design.md` §12.4 / §6 candidate (c), and it is deliberately not done:
// the standing rule is to emit uniform WasmGC and let Heap2Local scalarize.
//
// A count that goes DOWN here is a real advance and wants that rep change written up. A
// count that goes UP means the payload boxes multiplied.
function tick(n: i32): i32 {
  let acc = 0
  let i = 0
  while i < n {
    let u: i64 | boolean = true
    if i % 2 == 0 { u = 7 }
    if u is i64 { acc = acc + (u as i32) }
    i = i + 1
  }
  return acc
}
print(tick(100))
